Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — source-developed working scene
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: Independent screenplay development under DEC-0019; refinements during production

INT. CAFE BESIDE A PARK - TUESDAY NOON

Jackie chose the café. Claire sits at another table. Elias waits outside. No investigator, camera or statement hidden inside a reunion.

12:06. Jackie enters in a green coat. Zoe stands.

JACKIE
If you run at me, I leave.

Zoe stops three feet away.

JACKIE
You can hug me.

They hold each other harder than planned. No roomful of applause. When they part, Jackie wipes her face first. Neither intends to concede that detail later.

JACKIE
Your hair is awful.

ZOE
So is yours.

JACKIE
Professionals.

ZOE
Criminal professionals?

JACKIE
Probably not.

They sit. Zoe's rehearsed speech abandons her.

ZOE
I'm sorry. I decided what you'd done before I knew.

JACKIE
I thought you kept choosing them. I hated you.

ZOE
I know.

Jackie turns a sugar packet between her fingers.

JACKIE
You didn't know.

ZOE
That doesn't make it nice. I'm still sorry.

Jackie nods once. Enough to keep talking. Pours sugar into an empty cup.

ZOE
You don't have a drink.

JACKIE
Preparing.

The waiter arrives. Jackie orders chips. Zoe judges silently, very visibly.

Later Jackie offers only what she chooses about the siblings. Her brother grew. One sister draws horses badly. The youngest still hates peas. Twelve minutes of video with her brother, four spent pretending the connection failed.

No address or placement detail.

JACKIE
I don't know if we'll live together. Contact first, maybe. If they're safe, maybe they stay.

ZOE
You don't need to know today.

JACKIE
Who are you?

ZOE
Shut up.

Jackie stares towards the park.

JACKIE
I don't want to be their mother again. I was six.

Zoe does not fill the silence with a promise to fix it.

JACKIE
I want to be their sister.

ZOE
You should get to.

Jackie looks back. Nods. Zoe steals a chip; Jackie slaps her hand.

At the other table Claire looks up at the ordinary noise, then leaves them to their conversation. This part of their friendship is not evidence somebody else needs to collect.
